ny types of files open  more than one view of them so for  example for markdown files you can also  open a rendered view and of course you  can  new books and one of the really nifty  things that Jew blab can do miss for  example if you start a new console it's  kind of like a command-line interface  you can actually attach it to the same  kernel basically the same instance of  Python notebook that you just opened it  itself is running you can put them side  by side and so if you define you know  variables in the notebook say the  command line of the terminal becomes a  web sorry the console becomes aware of  them so those variables exist here and  you can actually modify them and they're  modified in the notebook as well  so one thing that I've heard a lot of  requests for is a way to you know for a  lot of users used Jupiter Jupiter lab to  interact with their data they want to  you know dig into it they want to  analyze it but one thing that's kind of  awkward to do right now is you know  given that you've navigated to some you  know data resource in the file browser  it's somewhat difficult or awkward to  then actually consume that resource in  your notebook you're basically stuck  just writing the same Python code you  know for importing that data source that  you would be anywhere so it'd be nice if  there was a way to more closely  integrate the file browser with a  notebook and just like that the content  stack of do plyo in general but there  are some problems with making that  happen and the biggest maybe fundamental  difficulty is just the architecture of  Jupiter itself and this is true for both  notebook classic and for lab is that  it's split into kind of three parts so  there is the browser which is the client  that's the thing that you actually like  see and interact with there's the server  which is the thing that's actually  grabbing the files off of your  filesystem and then there's the kernel  which is the Python instance or whatever  that's actually running in the notebook  itself and these are three separate  things that not only do they not need to  be running on the same computer they can  actually be running on different  continents and sort of a more immediate  problem is that part of the way that  Jupiter has avoided dealing with  these difficult problems is to make it  so that whenever you request a file in  your client the server will immediately  actually dump fetch the eagerly fetch  the entire contents of that file back to  your client so there's like a large  remote data transfer at that point  before your browser will actually like  render anything and so if your I  personally you know for my research I  often worked with files that were like  terabytes in size so for something like  that this is completely impractical  another limitation is that the file  browser itself is tied to just whatever  local file system the server happens to  be running on and so what we'd like is  for it to be more flexible for  essentially anything that is a data  store or anything that sort of looks  like a file system you know no matter  whether it's living locally or living  remotely that you'd be able to browse  through it using the file browser and  this is not a new problem so these  issues have been brought up since the  beginning of the duper lab project three  or four years ago but until now there  haven't really been any like practical  implementations of solutions for them  and so one solution for one small part  of the problem and it stupid lab hdf5  which allows you to open and interact  with potentially very large hdf5 files  with the in jupiter lab itself so if  you're not familiar with hdf5 files  they're basically like they're kind of  like a mini files mini file system in a  single file so it's they have like  direct groups which are just like  directories you know on your file system  and then they have datasets which are  just these like you know in simplest  terms like these tabular data sets 